WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.800
Critics spend their lives telling you which
movies are good and which movies are not so

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:04.300
good.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.360
But just because a movie's been savaged by
critical reception, that doesn't actually

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.620
mean it's not worth your time.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:14.420
Here are some surprisingly great, but critically
hated, movies you can watch on Netflix right

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:14.920
now.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.560
National Treasure

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.820
"I'm gonna steal it."

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.300
"What?"

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.800
"I'm going to steal the declaration of independence."

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.500
National Treasure feels like someone threw
Indiana Jones in a blender with The Da Vinci

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.750
Code and then added some Nicolas Cage strangeness
on top.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.340
Cage stars as the ridiculously-named Benjamin
Franklin Gates, a treasure hunter who has

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.720
to decode a series of historical clues that
will lead him to a long-buried treasure hidden

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.600
by the Freemasons.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.300
Despite the high concept and a packed cast
of talented actors, the film only managed

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.040
a 44 percent critic rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.780
Despite that, National Treasure is a hoot,
with laugh-out loud dialogue and some surprisingly

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.390
accurate historical facts.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.580
Well, besides all that stuff about hidden
Freemason treasure, that is.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.820
"On the back of a hundred-dollar bill is an
etching of Independence Hall based on a painting

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:11.060
done in the 1780s who, and the artist was
actually a friend of Benjamin Franklin's!

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.260
It's wonderful."

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:13.680
Mascots

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.450
Christopher Guest has been a part of some
of the best comedy films of the last 40 years,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.649
from Best in Show to This Is Spinal Tap, and
his faux-documentary comedic stylings are

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:23.980
still hilarious today.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.840
"Why don't you just make 10 louder and make
10 be the top number and make that a little

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.340
louder?"

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.480
"These go to 11."

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.000
In short, the man is a comedy legend, so it's
no wonder that 2016's Mascots, his first feature-length

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
film in almost a decade, came with some high
expectations.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.890
The film follows a group of sports mascots
who gather for the Fluffies, an annual competition

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:49.820
and awards ceremony for mascotry.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.020
Unfortunately, the hype might have been too
much for the film, as indicated by its 50

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:55.400
percent Rotten Tomatoes rating.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:59.919
While it might not have delighted critics,
or even most audiences, the film's absolutely

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:00.680
worth a watch.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.560
It's packed to the brim with bizarre characters
played by some of the best comedic actors

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.260
in the business.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.680
“Bronze Fluffy, Silver Fluffy, and Gold
Fluffy.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.140
And believe me, every mascot knows just how
important those awards can be."

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:15.520
Wet Hot American Summer

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.320
Some films become so ingrained in popular
culture that it's hard to remember they weren't

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.240
box office smashes or critical darlings when
they came out.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.880
Wet Hot American Summer is a perfect example.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.000
The cast list alone should encourage you to
see it — it's led by a roster of comedy

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.390
all-stars that includes Paul Rudd, Ken Marino,
Amy Poehler, Bradley Cooper, Elizabeth Banks,

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.380
H. Jon Benjamin and many more.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.240
"You taste like a burger.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:45.920
I don't like you anymore."

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:51.040
Unfortunately, critics couldn't quite click
with the film, giving it a 33 percent on Rotten

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:51.540
Tomatoes.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.800
Still, Wet Hot American Summer was ahead of
its time, and you should absolutely check

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.140
out this comedy classic.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.280
"I'd like to take this opportunity to thank
you all for making the last eight weeks, without

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.920
rival, the best summer of my entire life."

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.880
Trailer Park Boys: The Movie

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.790
An adaptation of the long-running Canadian
comedy show of the same name, Trailer Park

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.930
Boys: The Movie follows three friends in a
trailer park as they try to get rich through

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.640
petty crime.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.440
The joy of the show has always been about
watching the natural chemistry between the

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.640
characters, and the movie extends that simple
pleasure beautifully.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.280
"Julien, pull over, man, I gotta p---."

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.960
"Ricky, we're being pursued by the cops, no,
not gonna happen."

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:30.980
"I can't hold it.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:31.860
I can't.”

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.900
The movie is so great at creating a sense
of rhythm that you can't help but relax into

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.860
it, despite its negative critical reviews.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:40.840
"What's your friend doing?"

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.720
“Bubbles, don’t you move.”

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.860
Goon: Last of the Enforcers

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.180
The original Goon was a surprisingly engaging
sports movie with a sweet romance and some

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.500
genuinely great acting from Seann William
Scott in the lead role.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:01.600
Unfortunately, the sequel, Goon: Last of the
Enforcers, didn't fare quite as well critically,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.840
only managing to muster 42 percent at Rotten
Tomatoes.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.760
While it might not be as electrifying as the
first film, Last of the Enforcers is a capable

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.840
sequel, bringing back fan favorite characters
and featuring even more grotesque violence

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:14.340
on the ice.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.600
Through it all, Scott anchors the film as
the lovable, thick-headed Doug Glatt with

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.590
the same easy charisma and puppy-like enthusiasm
that he showed the first time out.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:24.800
"And that's when I got bit by the sellin’
insurance bug.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.280
She laid eggs right inside of me and now you're
here."

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:30.440
"I'm allergic to eggs."

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:31.780
"You're an idiot.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:32.529
Nobody's allergic to eggs."

